WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.250
Larry Arnn:
Senior Class President is Nicholas Brown.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:13.179
He comes from Columbus, he majored in economics.
He did very well. He didn't do as well as

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:22.039
say, Sarah, but he did really well. I get
to read a sentence that I didn't think I would

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:26.349
ever read about a student at Hillsdale College.
He is president of the entrepreneurship club

00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:33.530
and he does a lot of volunteering and he's
an incredibly organized kid, but also he co-owned

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:38.829
and operated ... I'm just reading this, because
I can't believe it ... Beechwood Avenue Limited,

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:47.089
a startup clothing design firm that manufactures
and sells upscale men's attire, and this lasted

00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:53.170
for a year and four months, which means I
guess it went bust after that time. Isn't

00:00:53.170 --> 00:01:03.850
it like a dog walking on its hind legs? The
miracle was it was done at all.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:11.619
Nick is among the senior class officers. He's
a very artful kid. Nick, are you walking up

00:01:11.619 --> 00:01:19.780
here? Start walking. These kids came loaded
for  this year, and it

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:26.420
wasn't like the year Morel graduated when
they wanted Chuck Norris. Clarence Thomas

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:33.070
has been in the top five on the senior class
list to be commencement speaker for the sixteen

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:37.460
years that I have been here. I have never
been willing to invite him, and the reason

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:46.750
is I know how busy he is, and I've always
regarded it as an imposition. As you can tell,

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.600
I wish to be of service to him, rather than
the other way around. I've always told him

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.039
no, but this year he showed up, and Waylon
helped him, I think, with the argument that

00:01:56.039 --> 00:02:08.009
perhaps Justice Thomas is offended that you
have not asked him.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:15.780
That is sophism taken to a high pitch. Can
you imagine the poor Justice sitting there

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.810
in his chambers in the Supreme Court worrying
whether I'm going to ask him to come here

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:25.700
and to come here and?
I smiled when they said that, and I said,

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:32.400
"Okay, that's pretty good." I said, "I will
write that in the letter to him," and I did,

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:43.440
so we all owe a very great debt to Senior
Class President Nicholas Brown.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:58.240
Nick Brown:
Good afternoon. Thank you to Dr. Arnn, Justice

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:06.390
Thomas, the rest of the platform party, faculty
and staff, parents and friends. Without you,

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:13.010
this wouldn't the celebration it ought to
be. To my fellow graduates, congratulations.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:19.790
I think we finally made Hillsdale great again.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:29.660
It truly is an honor to stand before you today,
and I thank you for that. When I first visited

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:36.400
Hillsdale College, it was President's Day
2012. To celebrate, winter brought the only

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:43.060
gift it could, with one of the worst blizzards
the college had ever suffered. Dr. Arnn addressed

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:47.720
those of us who would come anyway. He said,
"We put the college in Michigan, because how

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:56.030
would you get the work done if you lived somewhere
warm?" That struck me as a weird sales pitch,

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:58.350
but weirder yet, it worked.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:04.540
Now, for years later, no one here needs a
sales pitch for Hillsdale college, not at

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:11.180
this point, at least I hope not, but maybe
we could benefit from one final sales pitch

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:18.069
for the liberal arts, a defense of what we
just spent four years doing. In our age, western

00:04:18.069 --> 00:04:22.680
heritage has been replaced with multicultural
studies and the honor code with political

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:27.590
correctness, and the confusion has gotten
so bad, we don't even know where to go to

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:35.960
the bathroom, yet here, we continue to embrace
the wisdom of the past.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:41.289
Today, the liberal arts are under attack,
and Hillsdale College is the best defense

00:04:41.289 --> 00:04:48.319
it could hope for. For weeks and months, as
we approached this day, many have asked us

00:04:48.319 --> 00:04:54.020
the same question. "What are you going to
do?" We've heard it all of our lives. "What

00:04:54.020 --> 00:05:00.099
do you want to be when you grow up?" It used
to be possible and even cute to say a basketball

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:06.719
star, but among us, the only person that can
say that without people laughing is Kyle Cooper.

00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:14.270
The rest of us need a better answer. Now that
I'm all grown up, my answer is to be an entrepreneur.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:19.080
I can't think of a better preparation for
this than the one the liberal arts has given

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.870
me.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:25.090
I've heard it said that entrepreneurship is
living a few years of your life like most

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:32.199
people won't so that you can spend the rest
of your life like most people can't. The same

00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:38.389
could be said for the liberal arts. Our Hillsdale
education has allowed us to live unlike most

00:05:38.389 --> 00:05:43.949
other students. We have read the old books
and engaged in a dialogue which recognizes

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:49.560
that the answers are not easily found. We've
come to better understand ourselves, and we've

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:57.699
undertaken a profound inquiry. What we've
done is at its core entrepreneurial. Few of

00:05:57.699 --> 00:06:04.080
my classmates would consider themselves entrepreneurs,
yet for all of us, the last four years have

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.909
been an entrepreneurial pursuit as we questioned,
analyzed, and searched.

00:06:09.909 --> 00:06:16.740
The word "entrepreneur" comes from a thirteenth
century French verb "entreprendre," meaning

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:22.729
to do something or to undertake. In Dr. Steele's
"History of Economic Thought", we learn that

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:30.659
the first academic use of the word was likely
in 1730 by the economist Richard Cantillon.

00:06:30.659 --> 00:06:34.449
He identified the willingness to bear the
financial risk of a business venture as the

00:06:34.449 --> 00:06:40.740
defining characteristic of an entrepreneur.
This willingness to bear is essential for

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:46.439
any successful entrepreneur, but also for
the student of the liberal arts.

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:52.749
For the last four years, we have struggled
under the brilliance of the great thinkers.

00:06:52.749 --> 00:06:58.639
The work which accompanied our pursuit was
hard, but the weight of the practice itself

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:03.800
was also silently present. Whether we recognized
it or not, the pursuit of the liberal arts

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:10.439
presents real danger to students, as we question
our perceived reality, evaluating the morays

00:07:10.439 --> 00:07:16.249
which root us, we run the risk of being deceived
by sophistry.

00:07:16.249 --> 00:07:20.689
Plato's "Protagoras" might be helpful here,
partly because I'm still trying to impress

00:07:20.689 --> 00:07:27.949
Dr. Arnn, but mostly because Plato says something
worth knowing. "For I tell you, there is far

00:07:27.949 --> 00:07:33.289
more serious risk in the purchase of doctrines
than in that of edibles. When you buy vitals

00:07:33.289 --> 00:07:38.139
and liquors, you can take the advice of an
expert as to what is fit to eat or drink and

00:07:38.139 --> 00:07:43.879
what is not, but you cannot carry away doctrines
in a separate vessel. You are compelled when

00:07:43.879 --> 00:07:49.449
you handed over the price to take the doctrine
in your very soul by learning it, and so to

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:52.059
depart either an injured or a benefited man."

00:07:52.059 --> 00:08:01.199
The kind of education we have embraced has
been rigorous, unrelenting, and even incendiary.

00:08:01.199 --> 00:08:06.400
Pursued in solitude, it had the potential
to consume us and to leave us the slaves of

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:13.610
deceptive ideologies. Done correctly, it required
a partnership to sift through the appeals

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:20.900
of the great thinkers as we questioned the
legitimacy of their claims. In cooperation,

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:25.210
we chose to bear the burden of the liberal
arts because of all the questions it compels

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:27.839
us to ask.

00:08:27.839 --> 00:08:34.229
Cantillon shows us that both entrepreneurship
and the liberal arts are inherently risky.

00:08:34.229 --> 00:08:38.570
Yet another economist suggests the passion
for discovery moves both the entrepreneur

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:44.530
and the student to embrace this risk. For
Israel Kirzner, entrepreneurial discovery

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:50.940
gradually, but systematically, pushes back
the boundaries of sheer ignorance. Just as

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:56.450
the entrepreneur dissolves his ignorance in
the discovery of hidden opportunities, our

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:02.100
embrace of the liberal arts erases our ignorance,
driving us ever closer to a knowledge of the

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.660
truth.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:09.190
At college, we grappled with the nature of
man, his purpose, and his role in the governance

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:15.770
of our society. We examined the concept of
justice and what it looked like rightly applied.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:21.060
We questioned the efficacy of our modern world,
and we asked for advice from the greatest

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:28.240
thinkers of our heritage. In that rare instance,
what we thought we knew as proven true, though

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:35.040
more often, in a moment of disbelief, our
assumptions were exposed to be woefully inadequate.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.190
For Kirzner, that surprise, which accompanies
the realization that one has overlooked something

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:47.080
in fact readily available, defines entrepreneurial
discovery, and it also encapsulates the liberal

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:54.370
arts. When viewed as an act of entrepreneurship,
our education begins as an embrace of introspection

00:09:54.370 --> 00:10:01.250
aimed towards discovery and culminates in
an unexpected moment of awareness.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:06.580
In this moment, when our ignorance is found
only to disappear, entrepreneurship and the

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:13.250
liberal arts arrive at their greatest potential.
No longer is either practice solitary, but

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:20.290
instead the focus now turns outward. As the
bible says in Galatians 5:13, "You, my brothers

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:25.210
and sisters, were called to be free, but do
not use your freedom to indulge the flesh.

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:31.810
Rather, serve one another humbly in love."
To some, entrepreneurship and the liberal

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:37.900
arts seem like selfish acts. The entrepreneur
innovates for profit, and the student seeks

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:45.370
after knowledge, but in these individual pursuits,
something amazing happens. When the discovery

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:50.530
of opportunity is followed by action, we begin
to serve others.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:55.590
For the entrepreneur to profit, he must innovate,
creating a solution to the problem he has

00:10:55.590 --> 00:11:00.700
encountered. He puts his discovered knowledge
to work by improving the lives of others,

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:05.890
and the student of the liberal arts can do
the same. In our study of the good, the true,

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:11.310
and the beautiful, we have inherited a unique
opportunity to apply our discovery to the

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:16.690
pursuit of our life. In everything that we
do, if we embrace the liberal arts as our

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:23.240
guide post, we become equipped to lead those
who are lost. The education, then, which is

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:30.600
truly enlightened, is not content simply to
contemplate, but rather aims to put contemplation

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.690
into practice. When we unite the revealed
truth of the liberal arts with our vocation,

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:39.580
our education equips us for influence.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:44.060
In Shakespeare's "The Tempest" ... This one's
for you, Dr. Whalen ... Prospero serves as

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:49.190
a warning to the one who abandons his duty
to others for his zealous pursuit of the contemplative

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:54.760
of life, "I, thus neglecting worldly ends,
all dedicated to closeness and the bettering

00:11:54.760 --> 00:12:00.570
of my mind with that which, but by being so
retired, o'erprized all popular rate, in my

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:06.250
false brother awakened an evil nature, and
my trust, like a good parent, did beget of

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:13.300
him a falsehood in its contrary as great as
my trust was, which had indeed no limit, a

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:16.810
confidence sans bound."

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:21.130
Prospero's selfish pursuit of knowledge came
with a cost. The avocation of his duty to

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:29.160
rule brought only hardship, leaving his nation
exposed and his family exiled. To pursue knowledge

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:36.040
for itself can leave one alone, absent, and
eventually insignificant. Our education then

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:41.590
must pursue knowledge with an appeal to our
greater purpose. Economics has provided me

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:46.210
with an understanding of how the world works,
but literature and humanities have shown us

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:51.330
what the world ought to be. The beauty of
the liberal arts is found in this dialogue

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:55.110
between what is and what could be.

00:12:55.110 --> 00:13:01.380
As we leave the seclusion of coffee breaks
at AJ's and lectures on monopoly markets,

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:06.290
we must be reminded that there's work to be
done. The world increasingly embraces all

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:13.660
that is wrong, false, and ugly. We are among
the lonely few who have all the right questions.

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:19.130
None of us made a mistake in embracing the
liberal arts. To those who suggest otherwise,

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:23.980
ask them, "What is the good?" My friends,
we can do that now.

